{"id": "20479730", "revid": "49675352", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki?curid=20479730", "title": "Norwegian Institute for Social Research", "text": "The Institute for Social Research (, ISF) is an independent social science research institute based in Oslo, Norway.\nIt was founded in 1950 by Vilhelm Aubert, Arne N\u00e6ss, Eirik Rinde, and Stein Rokkan. It publishes the journal \"Tidsskrift for samfunnsforskning\".\nThe institute is organized into three research groups, each with its own research director. They are Politics, democracy and civil society (led by Johannes Bergh), Working life and welfare (led by Kjersti Misje \u00d8stbakken), and Gender equality, integration and migration (led by Jan-Paul Brekke). In addition, the institute has two affiliated research centers: Center for Research on Voluntary Sector and Civil Society and CORE - Center for Research on Gender Equality. In total, the institute has 65 employees."}
